---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import ModelViewer from '../components/ModelViewer.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Lab"
  description="Interactive 3D and exploded-view assemblies — the viewer that project pages will use for CAD."
>
  <div class="wrap head">
    <p class="eyebrow">Lab</p>
    <h1>Interactive assemblies</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      A drawing shows you what a thing looks like. An exploded view shows you how it goes
      together — which is the part that actually matters when someone else has to build it.
      This is the viewer the project pages use, running on a placeholder assembly until the
      real CAD is exported.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="wrap">
    <ModelViewer demo caption="Placeholder controller stack — drag to orbit, slide to explode" height="520px" />
  </div>

  <div class="wrap wrap-narrow prose body">
    <h2>How it works</h2>
    <p>
      The viewer loads glTF/GLB models and separates them along each part's own centroid axis,
      so an assembly modelled in SOLIDWORKS and exported with one top-level object per part
      explodes correctly without any per-model configuration.
    </p>
    <p>
      three.js is code-split into its own bundle and only fetched once a viewer scrolls into
      range, and the render loop pauses whenever the canvas is off screen. Pages without a model
      ship no 3D code at all — which is why the rest of this site stays fast despite carrying a
      WebGL renderer.
    </p>

    <h2>Adding a model</h2>
    <p>
      Drop a <code>.glb</code> into <code>public/models/</code> and add it to a project's frontmatter:
    </p>
    <pre><code>model:
  src: /models/smart-coffee.glb
  caption: Controller stack, exploded
  explode: 1.6</code></pre>
    <p>
      Export each part you want to separate as its own top-level object. Nested children move
      with their parent, so a sub-assembly that should stay together can simply be grouped.
    </p>

    <h2>Next</h2>
    <p>
      The plan is to bring the mechanical work onto the site properly — enclosure assemblies,
      the curtain rail brackets, the Pi rack, and the coffee machine's touch panel, each as a
      model you can take apart rather than a photograph of a finished object. Interactive
      schematics and dimensioned drawings are the other half of that.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .head {
    padding-block: clamp(2.5rem, 6vw, 4rem) 0;
  }

  .lede {
    max-width: 62ch;
    color: var(--text-dim);
    margin-top: 1rem;
    font-size: 1.03rem;
  }

  .body {
    margin-top: 3.5rem;
  }

  pre {
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    padding: 1rem 1.15rem;
    overflow-x: auto;
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  pre code {
    background: none;
    border: 0;
    padding: 0;
    font-size: inherit;
  }
</style>
